The Elastic Net algorithm is a regularization method used to overcome the challenge of dealing with correlated features in high-dimensional datasets. It is a linear regression model that combines the L1 and L2 regularization methods. The mathematical formula used for Elastic Net is as follows:

$ElasticNet = \sum_{i=1}^{n}(y_{i} - \hat{y_{i}})^{2} + \lambda_{1}\sum_{j=1}^{p}|\beta_{j}| + \lambda_{2}\sum_{j=1}^{p}\beta_{j}^{2}$

where:

- n is the number of observations
- p is the number of features
- y is the response variable
- $\hat{y}$ is the predicted value of y
- $\beta$ is the coefficient for each feature
- $\lambda_{1}$ and $\lambda_{2}$ are hyperparameters that control the strength of the L1 and L2 regularization terms, respectively

The penalty term combining L1 and L2 norms contributes to sparsity by shrinking the coefficient estimates towards zero. The L1 penalty, also known as Lasso, sets many of the coefficients to zero, creating a sparse model. The L2 penalty, also known as Ridge, shrinks the coefficients towards zero, but does not necessarily set them to zero, leading to a less sparse model. The combination of these penalties in the Elastic Net algorithm provides a balance between sparsity and accuracy, making it a suitable method for datasets with correlated features.

Elastic Net outperforms Lasso when dealing with highly correlated features since it is able to select multiple correlated features at once, whereas Lasso tends to select only one of them. This makes Elastic Net more robust and stable than Lasso in such scenarios.

Here is an example of using the ElasticNet module in scikit-learn to fit a linear regression model on a given dataset with correlated features:

```php
use Phpml\Regression\ElasticNet;

// Load the dataset
$dataset = new CsvDataset('data.csv', 50, true);

// Split the dataset into training and test sets
$split = new StratifiedRandomSplit($dataset, 0.2);
$trainingSamples = $split->getTrainSamples();
$trainingLabels = $split->getTrainLabels();
$testSamples = $split->getTestSamples();
$testLabels = $split->getTestLabels();

// Fit the model using Elastic Net
$regression = new ElasticNet(0.1, 0.5);
$regression->train($trainingSamples, $trainingLabels);

// Predict the test set labels
$predicted = $regression->predict($testSamples);

// Evaluate the model using mean squared error
$metric = new MeanSquaredError();
$score = $metric->score($testLabels, $predicted);
echo "Mean Squared Error: " . $score;
```

To modify the existing code of Elastic Net to apply it to a dataset with 50 features and 1000 observations, where 20% of the features are highly correlated and the rest of the features have low correlations, we can use cross-validation to tune the hyperparameters and report the mean squared error of the model on the test set:

```php
use Phpml\Regression\ElasticNet;
use Phpml\ModelSelection\StratifiedRandomSplit;
use Phpml\Metric\MeanSquaredError;
use Phpml\CrossValidation\GridSearch;
use Phpml\Dataset\CsvDataset;

// Load the dataset
$dataset = new CsvDataset('data.csv', 50, true);

// Split the dataset into training and test sets
$split = new StratifiedRandomSplit($dataset, 0.2);
$trainingSamples = $split->getTrainSamples();
$trainingLabels = $split->getTrainLabels();
$testSamples = $split->getTestSamples();
$testLabels = $split->getTestLabels();

// Define the hyperparameter grid
$alphas = [0.01, 0.1, 1];
$l1_ratios = [0.1, 0.5, 0.9];
$params = ['alpha' => $alphas, 'l1_ratio' => $l1_ratios];

// Perform grid search using cross-validation
$search = new GridSearch(new ElasticNet(), $params);
$search->fit($trainingSamples, $trainingLabels);

// Get the best hyperparameters and fit the model using them
$bestParams = $search->getBestParameters();
$regression = new ElasticNet($bestParams['alpha'], $bestParams['l1_ratio']);
$regression->train($trainingSamples, $trainingLabels);

// Predict the test set labels
$predicted = $regression->predict($testSamples);

// Evaluate the model using mean squared error
$metric = new MeanSquaredError();
$score = $metric->score($testLabels, $predicted);
echo "Mean Squared Error: " . $score;
```

The above code performs a grid search using cross-validation to find the best hyperparameters (alpha and l1_ratio) for Elastic Net. It then fits the model using the best hyperparameters and predicts the test set labels, before evaluating the model using mean squared error.

